  "title": "Last Stone Weight",
  "topic": "heap",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "heap / Max simulate",
  "statement": "# Last Stone Weight\n\nA row of stones is given, where `stones[i]` is the weight of stone `i`. The game repeats the following round while more than one stone remains: pick the two heaviest stones `x <= y`, smash them together, and if `x < y` put a new stone of weight `y - x` back into the row. Equal stones destroy each other completely.\n\nA single surviving stone keeps its weight; when every stone is destroyed the result is `0`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= stones.length <= 30` and `1 <= stones[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "stones = [2, 7, 4, 1, 8, 1]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Smash 8+7 -> 1 ([2,4,1,1,1]), then 4+2 -> 2 ([1,1,1,2]), then 2+1 -> 1 ([1,1,1]), then 1+1 -> 0 ([1]): one stone of weight 1 survives."
    },
    {
      "input": "stones = [2, 2]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "The equal pair destroys itself completely, leaving nothing: 0."
    },
    {
      "input": "stones = [1]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "A single stone never plays a round and keeps its weight 1."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function lastStoneWeight(stones: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Every round needs the two current maximums; the rest of the row is irrelevant.",
    "A max-heap delivers the two heaviest stones without re-sorting the row.",
    "Push the difference back only when the two weights differ."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Repeatedly smash the two heaviest stones, returning any remainder to the row. A max-heap always exposes the two heaviest in logarithmic time.",
    "why_optimal": "Heap extraction replaces the full re-sort of every round, cutting each round from linearithmic to logarithmic time. On 42 generated cases PAF measured the brute force at 0.47 ms and the optimal at 0.67 ms.",
    "pitfalls": "Equal stones vanish without a remainder; only push y - x back when the weights differ. A single starting stone is already the answer."
  },
